# HSM collector conformance v1 — the table connection of the collector session (#1846 part 2, wire contract
# "Table connection").
#
# The collector is on a session to the driver's SCRIPTED SESSION SERVER (create_collector_on_session, see
# session_transport_contract.hsmtest), which also takes the table connection: the upgrade with hsm.tables.v1 and
# X-Hsm-Session, the table hello, the probe, binary block frames, one text ack each. Without a script the server stores
# a block, and answers a block of a producer and sequence it stored before with "duplicate", as the real server does.
# When the session ends the server closes its table connection with 1000, as the real server does.
#
# The managed collector implements it (4.1.0), the native collector since 0.15.1 (#1848 part 4; its driver runs the
# fixture in builds with the session transport and table sensors).
#
# No case is decided by a duration; a timeout in a verb only fails a case. A tick waits in the delivery, and a put that
# finds no table connection waits for one only for a while (SessionTimings.TableConnectionWait, which a driver may
# shorten) and then leaves its block at the head with its sequence. So a case waits for the state it needs:
# table_wait_connection_up before every tick that must send, and after every connection the script ends (a close, a
# protocol error, a dropped session, an ack that never comes) the case waits for the next connection and ticks again.
# The second tick sends the block if the first tick's put gave up before the next connection was open, and nothing if
# it was already sent there: the frames are the same either way. The wait for an ack ends as "no ack in time" when the
# scripted server has decided not to answer that frame (store_without_ack, no_answer), never by the request timeout.
#
# The table verbs of table_sensor_contract.hsmtest work on a session collector too, with the scripted server in place of
# the recording sender's table channel: table_server_frame_limit (the table hello's maxFrameBytes),
# table_server_answer_next, table_server_refuse_table_next, expect_table_block_attempts (block frames received),
# expect_table_blocks_within, expect_table_sequences, expect_table_sequences_increasing, expect_table_rows,
# expect_table_row (rows of stored blocks, refused tables left out). The registration verbs need the recording sender.
#
# table_server_answer_next on a session takes, besides stored, duplicate, all_refused, invalid (400) and
# not_delivered (503 with retryAfter 0):
#   store_without_ack   stored, no ack: the collector's resend is answered duplicate
#   no_answer           not stored, no ack
#   close_1013          the connection closed with 1013 instead of an answer; not stored
#   drop_session        the session cut without a close handshake (the server then closes the table connection with
#                       1000 "its session ended"); not stored
#   binary_frame        a binary frame instead of an answer (the collector closes with 1003)
#   garbage_text        a text that is not JSON instead of an answer (the collector closes with 1007)
#
# New verbs:
# table_register|path|timeout_s                    table_tick until the sensor is no longer pending (the session comes
#                                                  up in the background); fails at the timeout
# table_wait_connection_up|count|timeout_s         waits until the collector's table connection number `count` (1 = the
#                                                  first it opened) is open, its hello read and its probe answered, so
#                                                  a put sends on it at once; then checks that exactly `count` were
#                                                  opened. Judged by the collector's own state, not by the server's
#                                                  count of upgrades; fails at the timeout
# table_server_refuse_upgrades|status              every table upgrade is refused with this HTTP status
# table_server_accept_upgrades                     table upgrades are accepted again (the default)
# expect_table_connections|count|timeout_s         table upgrades accepted so far equal count (polled; 0 checks once)
# expect_refused_table_upgrades_at_least|count|timeout_s
# expect_table_upgrade_names_session|upgrade|session  table upgrade number `upgrade` (0 = first, refused ones counted)
#                                                  named in X-Hsm-Session the sessionId of session number `session`
# expect_table_probe_before_blocks                 every table connection that carried a block had a probe first
# expect_table_frame_connections|c0,c1,...         the table connection (1 = first accepted) of every block frame
# expect_table_close_code|code|timeout_s           the collector closed a table connection with this code
# expect_table_closed_before_session|timeout_s     the collector closed its table connection, with 1000, before it
#                                                  closed its session. Judged by the collector's own state at the
#                                                  moment its close of the session reaches the server (the table
#                                                  connection is down by then), never by the order in which the server
#                                                  reads the close frames of two sockets

